  "query_preview": "(Today through Friday)...\nIssued at 313 AM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nTODAY AND TONIGHT\n\nA remnant outflow boundary from yesterday that triggered widely \nscattered convection through the early overnight continues to linger \nacross the region, though it has washed out somewhat. A subtle \nshortwave disturbance aloft will slowly pass through the region \ntoday, and this may combine with the presently warm and very humid \nairmass to produce additional isolated to widely scattered \nconvection, mainly this afternoon into early evening, before \nsubsidence behind the weak wave and slightly drier air moving into \nthe area with surface high pressure combine to bring at least a \nbrief end to rain chances.\n\nA cap of varying strengths is noted in forecast soundings centered \naround 850 mb, and this along with relatively weak shear will be the \nprimary limiting factor on storms today. An isolated weak downburst \nor small hail will certainly be possible in any storms that can \nsustain themselves, but the primary concern will be heavy downpours \nand localized flooding, particularly in areas that have received \nsignificant rainfall in the past several days.\n\nHighs today should be at or just below persistence today, in the mid \nto upper 80s, with a 90 degree reading not out of the question \nsouth. The slightly drier air will allow lows tonight to drop down \ninto the 60s across the area.\n\nSurface high pressure will produce light to calm winds tonight, and \nexpect areas of fog, perhaps dense in spots, to form late in the \nevening into the overnight hours.\n\nSUNDAY ONWARD\n\nBroadly speaking, warm and humid conditions will continue through \nmid week, with some moderation in daytime highs late in the week, as \na broad upper level ridge persists from the southeastern into the \nwestern CONUS. The hottest conditions will be early in the week, \nwith highs Sunday through at least Tuesday well into the 80s or low \n90s across central Indiana, and heat index values possibly peaking \nin the mid 90s to low 100s each day, though convection potential \nincreases the uncertainty with respect to this. While we appear \nunlikely to reach strict heat advisory criteria at any point, at \nleast moderate heat risk is likely through mid week, and major heat \nrisk is likely to nudge into the area on one or more days, though \nagain, convection significantly complicates predictability of this.\n\nThunderstorms will be a threat for at least portions of the area \neach day, though PoPs will be highest Tuesday into Thursday, when a \nweak surface front and more substantial upper level disturbances on \nthe periphery of the ridge come into play.\n\nThere will be at least a low threat for severe storms each day from \nSunday through Wednesday, with significant uncertainty with respect \nto exact areas and timing owing to the inherent low predictability \nof weakly forced warm season convection, which is largely \nthermodynamically/cold pool driven. In any case, damaging winds will \nbe the primary severe threat, with of course a continued concern for \nheavy downpours and localized flooding as highly efficient rain \nrates will remain a concern with seasonably high freezing levels and \nprecipitable water values at or at times well above normal, and some \nareas seeing substantial rain in recent days.\n\nThough chances for showers and storms will persist beyond mid week \ninto the latter portion of the week, a more moderate airmass will \ncut down on available instability and result in more limited severe \nthreat beyond mid week. Run to run model consistency in recent days \nhas been somewhat poor, though, so stay tuned in this regard.",
